WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.300
Throughout the Saw franchise,

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.850
there have been many symbols and images

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.090
associated with Jigsaw,

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.060
from puzzle pieces, to
spirals, to the pigs,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.570
which are hidden in
many of the crime scenes

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:09.500
in this ninth movie.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.470
The very first victim is
targeted at a carnival,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.610
where you can spot this
pig on the funhouse mural,

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.230
and a man holding a pig balloon.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.440
The meaning behind the pig symbol

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.060
has changed a couple of times now.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.840
It started as a reference
to the Year of the Pig

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:20.780
in the Chinese Zodiac,

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.640
which was representative
of the Eastern ideas

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.600
that Jigsaw incorporated
into his teachings,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.730
and at some point, it became
a symbol of compassion

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.080
towards those in suffering.

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:32.880
JOHN: Did you know that pigs are
highly compassionate animals?

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.956
They show distress if
they see any other animal,

00:00:35.956 --> 00:00:38.620
including humans, suffering.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.630
- In Spiral, there's a new Jigsaw copycat,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:42.880
who has a new context for
the use of the pig symbol.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.520
It's a reference to the derogatory term

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.860
that people sometimes use
towards police officers.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.580
The new killer is targeting dirty cops,

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.400
and so, he ironically uses
the pig costume and symbol

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.775
to give them a taste of their own-

00:00:52.775 --> 00:00:53.608
- Brutality!

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:54.890
- And that's why the puppet avatar

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.780
has been changed this
time to a creepy pig.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.170
Perhaps the dirtiest police
officer, Detective Fitch,

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.570
even has a coffee mug
decorated with pig imagery.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.960
He discovers a pig mask shortly
before going into his trap,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.040
and a pig falls out of the
car when the police discover

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.980
where the killer
deposited Fitch's fingers.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.540
Other less noticeable pig symbols

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.480
include the pig models
displayed in the butcher shop

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.090
where one of the other
victims is discovered,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.060
including this adorable little pig chef,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.790
and the pig mask hiding in the background

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.030
as Zeke enters the church
to find Pete Dunleavy,

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.453
his number one suspect.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.170
To learn how each trap in Spiral

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.690
evolved from one of John Kramer's traps

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.070
in the original Saw movies,

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.130
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.223
This video is sponsored by Lionsgate.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.340
Spiral is the newest
chapter in the book of Saw,

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.190
and it's now available on
digital, Blu-Ray and 4K.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.690
If you watch my channel, you may remember

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:43.750
when I thought the Book of Saw

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.810
was going to be an
actual book in the movie.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.200
That didn't age too well.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.100
The movie stars Chris Rock, Max Minghella,

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.120
Marisol Nichols and Samuel L. Jackson.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.710
Darren Lynn Bousman, who
directed Saw 2, 3 and 4,

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:54.990
is back to direct Spiral,

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.270
and I noticed some references
to some of his other work

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.920
hidden in graffiti around the city.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.210
In addition to making movies,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.190
he's also done these immersive
theatrical experiences.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.820
Basically, expensive haunted
houses where you sign a waiver,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.410
but instead of the actors
trying to scare you,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.370
they just try to make
you really uncomfortable.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.340
His first one was in 2016 and
called The Tension Experience,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.270
and it's logo can be seen
graffitied on a pillar

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.500
at the 12th Street Bridge.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.810
The next year he followed that
up with The Lust Experience,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.540
whose logo is seen spray painted on a wall

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.430
at the courthouse.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.040
These experiences have their own lore,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.550
which is also referenced.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.110
BOS stands for Brotherhood of Seraph,

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.320
a group inside The Tension
Experience universe.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.930
The parcel delivery company
is OSDM, which stands for

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.650
Oracle Security and Data Management,

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.060
a fictional company featured
in The Lust Experience.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.780
As is par for the Saw franchise,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.320
there are plenty of other references

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.373
and hidden secrets to uncover,
so let's get into them.

00:02:46.061 --> 00:02:47.400
The movie opens with the normal,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.040
not-spooky Lionsgate logo.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.100
I guess the new Saw films

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.750
are just not going to do that anymore.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:52.583
How sad.

00:02:52.583 --> 00:02:54.100
Detective Marv Bozwick is lured away

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.580
from the 4th of July carnival
when he chases a purse thief.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.820
Ironically, stealing a purse
was a punishable offense

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.470
in the eyes of John Kramer in
the previous movie, Jigsaw.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.140
When Bozwick gets down
into the subway tunnel,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.760
he realizes he's been bamboozled

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.900
when his suspect has been
replaced by a mannequin.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.150
That could've been his first clue

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.080
that he was dealing with a Jigsaw copycat.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.990
You may have remembered
John's first ever workshop

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.070
in Saw 1 was in a mannequin factory.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.320
We come to learn that the new
copycat, Detective Schenk,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.980
believes in John Kramer's philosophy,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.940
but has his own ideas about
who it should be applied to.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.223
SCHENK: John Kramer was right. Spiral.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.460
Symbol of change, evolution, progress.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.400
But why limit that to an individual

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.500
when you can apply it to a whole system?

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.540
- So it makes sense that
a lot of Schenk's traps

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.940
seem to be evolutions of the Jigsaw traps.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.650
The subway trap is a lot like
the classroom trap from Saw 3.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.370
Each one requires the
victim to tear something

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.670
out of their body to escape
before time runs out.

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:45.200
There are also obvious parallels

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.230
to the trap from Jill's dream in Saw 7.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.360
And yes, I prefer to call
it Saw 7 instead of Saw 3D,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.220
so get used to it.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.530
But I'm not actually counting this one

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.020
as an inspiration for Schenk
because it was just a dream,

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.210
we have no proof that
it was ever a real trap.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.520
It is interesting how it
contains the Spiral logo, though.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.640
There are a couple of red
herrings that could be placed

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.890
to lead the audience into
thinking that certain characters

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.470
are responsible for the subway trap.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.200
Chief Banks seems to be a train fanatic,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.600
and decorates his apartment
with pictures of locomotives,

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.920
and the drug dealer
has a 9 and 3/4 tattoo,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.980
a reference to the famous
magical train platform

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:13.850
in Harry Potter.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.100
Boz is unable to escape
the subway trap in time,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.560
and the train that hits him
as a big IX on the front.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.930
The Saw films have always been
labeled with Roman numerals,

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.710
and this is the numeral
for the number nine.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.920
Spiral is the ninth film in the franchise.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.540
Then we meet Detective Zeke Banks,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.440
who is working undercover
with a gang of thieves,

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.000
who are in a heated
discussion about the fate

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:30.910
of Forrest Gump.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.310
- I ain't see no sequel. Where's Gump 2?

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:35.880
- It's almost like a statement to the fans

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.990
about Mark Hoffman.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.450
Hoffman was Jigsaw's successor,

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.230
the main villain of movies 4 through 7,

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.230
who was assumed dead
after being game over'd

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:43.200
in his last appearance.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.010
He didn't appear in the
next sequel, Jigsaw.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.650
This isn't a perfect analogy, though.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.710
For one, there is a
sequel to Forrest Gump,

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.580
the novel just never
got a movie adaptation

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.590
like the first one did.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.190
And for two, Josh Stolberg,

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.060
the co-screenwriter of Jigsaw and Spiral,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.450
who is probably the most likely
candidate to write Saw 10,

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.100
has publicly tweeted about the issue,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.030
stating that while he
didn't write the last movie

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.660
to include Hoffman, he personally
thinks that he is alive.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.920
Speaking of Hoffman,
you'll notice this isn't

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.670
the same police station that
Hoffman annihilated in Saw 7.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.810
That was the Metro PD 13th precinct,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.720
while this is the South
Metro Police Station.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.750
So we're dealing with a
different part of the same city,

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.900
but there is a nice reference
to the 13th precinct

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:17.733
in this movie.

00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:20.350
Perhaps both buildings were
created by the same architect,

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.810
because the evidence room in Spiral

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.830
has a very similar layout to
the evidence room from Saw 7.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.620
Detective Banks gets
the call to investigate

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:27.500
the train accident.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.850
It's impossible to say for sure,

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.530
but is that Saw 1 director
James Wan sitting by the window

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.363
on the train?

00:05:32.363 --> 00:05:33.880
One interesting thing about Spiral is that

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.640
nearly every previous Saw
director was involved in some way.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.340
James Wan was an executive producer,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.690
Darren Lynn Bousman directed it,

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.330
David Hackl was a visual consultant,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.420
and Kevin Gruetert was
an executive producer.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.720
That leaves the Spierig
brothers, who directed Jigsaw,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.090
as the only Saw directors
not to work on Spiral.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.190
However, I did find a
detail that may in fact

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.930
be a massive Jigsaw connection.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.320
It comes when Zeke is
interrogating Bozwick's wife.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.153
Roll it.

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.000
ZEKE: In the last few weeks,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.573
did you notice anything unusual?

00:06:00.573 --> 00:06:02.573
KARA: There was one thing,
there was a black SUV

00:06:02.573 --> 00:06:03.573
parked outside

00:06:03.573 --> 00:06:04.550
one night last week.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.010
And he went out, and he
walked up, and I don't know,

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.740
the next thing I know, he was
yelling at somebody inside.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.570
- This is never brought
up again in the movie,

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.420
which I find a little strange.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.530
Jigsaw also had unanswered
questions like this.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.390
For example, when the
pathologist, Eleanor,

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.480
makes it out of the pig
farm, she waves down a car,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.780
then looks concerned as
she sees who's driving it.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.160
We can't really tell if it's black,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.520
but it does look to be
roughly the size of an SUV

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.600
Nelson also drives a gray SUV,
it could look black at night.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.750
Maybe a future sequel
will provide the answer.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:32.890
We never really get any follow-up

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.340
about Zeke's wife and kid, either,

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.600
just as Logan Nelson's kid

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.960
was a seemingly unfinished
storyline in Jigsaw.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.250
As the police realize
they've got a Jigsaw copycat

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.590
on their hands, they study what
they know about John Kramer,

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.540
including this screenshot from Saw 4,

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.200
mugshots of Obi Tate and
Addison Corday from Saw 2,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.440
magazine and newspaper
articles from various movies,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.610
primarily Saw 4,

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.140
this photo of John, which is from

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.540
the Journal of Civil
Engineering, first seen in Saw 4,

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.400
and this screenshot of Billy the Puppet,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.320
which is from the classroom trap in Saw 3.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.860
In addition to all of that,
the cork boards contain maps

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.030
and photos from the night of
Detective Bozwick's death,

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.340
and photos of what appears
to be another crime scene

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.270
involving a knife.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.160
This may in fact be a
leftover from a scene

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.300
that was cut from the movie.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.230
Apparently this trap
involved someone's face

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.970
getting cut off, and they
had to submit the film to

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.690
the Motion Picture Association
11 times just to get it down

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.550
to an R-rating.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.200
One thing I've always had fun pointing out

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.330
in my previous Saw franchise
Things You Missed episodes

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.790
is the crew referenced easter eggs,

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.160
and this scene has a few of them.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.030
The police search Twitter for photos taken

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.670
in Stewart's Point that night for anything

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.430
that might help them to
identify the suspect.

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:31.120
Noel99 and NinaC aren't crew members,

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.240
but remember those names,
I'll come back to them later.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:34.890
The first one is Rafael Pacheco,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.240
a visual effects coordinator.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.870
In the corner, there's a
prompt to follow a user

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.710
named Kiki Singh, who's probably
related to Spiral's editor,

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:41.543
Dev Singh.

00:07:41.543 --> 00:07:43.850
Just a side note, I love the
idea that police work is done

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.560
by just Googling phrases related
to the date of the crime.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.477
He just has tabs open with
searches for, "July 4 night",

00:07:49.477 --> 00:07:51.810
"July 4 event", and "July 4 case",

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.360
as if somebody has already solved the case

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.150
and just posted the answers online.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.400
We also get a little
glimpse of his email inbox,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.130
where he's had exchanges with Jon C,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.890
which is likely visual effects
supervisor Jon Campfens.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.450
There are more names to spot
on Detective Drury's tablet,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.610
like Nina Campfens and Noel Campfens.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:07.640
They aren't listed in the credits,

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.600
but they're probably related
to VFX supervisor Jon Campfens.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.090
You'll also see Beau Parsons.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.890
Beau is another visual effects supervisor,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:17.010
and Shawn Morris, special
effects technician, no relation.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.040
The next police officer
to find himself in a trap

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.920
is Detective Fitch, who becomes the victim

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.975
of the finger trap.

00:08:22.975 --> 00:08:25.280
- Help! Help me!

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.560
- If each trap is inspired
by one of the original traps

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:28.570
created by John Kramer,

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:30.980
this one definitely draws
from the electric bathtub

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.830
from Saw 5, with the source of the danger

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:34.980
being the combination of
water and electricity.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.290
It's possible that this was
foreshadowed in the opening shot

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:38.900
of the movie, where one
of the carnival games

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:40.690
offers Pokemon plushies as prizes.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:43.060
Specifically, you can
win Squirtle or Pikachu,

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:46.060
the water type and the electric
type Pokemon, respectively.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.770
The ferris wheel was even blue and yellow.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.700
Amanda would be rocking
out if she was still here.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.883
The finger trap begins at
the sound of the buzzer,

00:08:51.883 --> 00:08:54.030
(BUZZ)

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.430
which sounds exactly like the
buzzer from the pig vat trap

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:57.375
in Saw 3.

00:08:57.375 --> 00:08:58.316
(BUZZ BUZZ)

00:08:58.316 --> 00:09:00.530
We hear the sound again later
on in the glass grinder trap,

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:01.830
where it's used to signal the beginning

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.160
of the flying glass shards.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.539
The finger trap is designed to take away

00:09:04.539 --> 00:09:06.460
Fitch's overactive trigger fingers.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.200
It's interesting to see an actual Saw trap

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.270
that takes away your fingers,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.840
because the poster for Saw 2 features

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:11.870
a pair of severed fingers,

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.960
but there's no finger-severing
scene in the movie,

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.530
so I guess it was a long time coming.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.460
The trap also takes place
at a location called

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.540
the Nesbitt Brothers' Breads.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.970
Michael Nesbitt is the movie's
assistant art director.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.190
Not having heard from his father,

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:23.930
Zeke goes to his
apartment to check on him.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.840
He comes across the old
photo of him and his dad

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.260
at Constantine's Hobby Shop.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.990
This is another crew reference easter egg.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:31.900
Jason Constantine was one of
Spiral's executive producers.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.960
A new package containing
another flash drive

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.750
is delivered to the police
station the next day,

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.730
and we get a short glimpse
of Zeke's computer screen

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:38.840
before he plugs it in.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.930
It seems he's looking up
an event from 11/11/2004,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.070
where two males got into
a fight in a parking lot.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.320
I don't think this report is
relevant to the story at all,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.660
as these descriptions don't match

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.880
any important Saw characters,
but I would note that

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:51.320
this would've been a matter of weeks after

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.730
the bathroom game in Saw 1.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:55.220
The date 11/11 is also
significant for the director

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.288
Darren Lynn Bousman, who
directed a horror film titled

00:09:57.288 --> 00:10:00.740
11/11/11, about strange
events occurring on that date.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.580
The clues lead Zeke and
Schenk to investigate

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.180
this meth dealer who may
have ties to their suspect.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.487
SING: Think we have enough
for a warrant, though?

00:10:09.487 --> 00:10:11.730
TAPP: Who said anything about a warrant?

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.423
SCHENK: Don't we need a warrant for this?

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:15.200
ZEKE: What're you talking about?

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:16.740
- The whole scene is
packed with easter eggs

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.260
from Saw 4 and 5.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.730
You can tell we're about
to get into a throwback

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.470
when they enter the
grimy apartment building

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:23.560
with the green tinge,
the signature aesthetic

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.040
of the first seven films.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.840
I'm first reminded of Officer Rigg's foray

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.840
into the Alexander Motel in Saw 4,

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.170
where he blows by a morbidly
obese man in a tank top

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:32.140
to go up the stairs.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:34.140
Here, we see Zeke and Schenk do the same.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:34.973
At the top of the stairs,

00:10:34.973 --> 00:10:36.800
there's a room completely
illuminated in red,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.570
just like the room where
Rigg finds the evidence

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:39.403
in the motel.

00:10:39.403 --> 00:10:41.050
On the way up the stairs,
Zeke is confronted

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.173
by an aggressive dog.

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:47.268
- Whoa, whoa, whoa! Shit! Get your dog!

00:10:48.393 --> 00:10:50.000
Get your fucking dog!

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.340
- This is almost like a perfect
reenactment of the scene

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.190
at Mark Hoffman's apartment in Saw 5.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:11:00.273
- Heel! Heel, I said! Don't provoke him!

00:11:00.273 --> 00:11:01.912
Asshole!

00:11:01.912 --> 00:11:05.000
Jesus Christ, come on, Peewee. Come on!

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.310
- Another Saw trope brought
back in Spiral is the idea

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:09.770
of the killer being disguised
as a victim of the game.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.180
In Saw 1 it was John,
in Saw 2 it was Amanda,

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.000
in Saw 4 it was Hoffman,
and in Jigsaw it was Nelson.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.930
This time, Detective Schenk
makes it appear as if

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.910
he's been killed, only to
reveal that he did this

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.240
by leaving his tattoo on
the real victim's arm.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.350
There's also a nice detail
in this scene, where they go

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:24.500
to interrogate Pete.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:26.090
Pete is the one who shot Schenk's father,

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:28.290
and Schenk stays back behind
his partner out of fear

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.810
of possibly being recognized,
even though he was just a kid

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:31.840
when they last met.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.200
The next victim is the
police captain, Angie Garza.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:35.840
She is trapped inside the evidence room by

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:36.860
the pig-masked Schenk.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.090
It makes sense, because only
a police officer would be able

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:40.110
to get into this room.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.540
The door to the evidence room is secured

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:43.350
by Vincent and Jules Safe Company.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.380
If that sounds familiar, it's
because Vincent and Jules

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:48.000
are the two main characters
in the 1994 crime movie,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:48.890
Pulp Fiction.

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.210
Jules was played by
actor Samuel L. Jackson,

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:53.430
who also plays Chief
Marcus Banks in Spiral.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.410
Marcus Banks is the father of Zeke Banks,

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.510
and the only character to
call him by his full name.

00:11:58.510 --> 00:11:59.343
- Ezekiel.

00:11:59.343 --> 00:12:00.740
- This may be a reference to Pulp Fiction,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.160
where one of the most famous scenes

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.430
features Samuel L. Jackson
quoting a Bible verse

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.490
called Ezekiel 25:17.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.760
Anyway, Captain Garza is
put into the wax trap,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:10.320
which will smother her face in hot wax

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.930
if she's unable to sever her spinal cord

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:13.080
with the blade under her neck.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:15.170
It's not really clear how
this trap works mechanically,

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.120
but there are a number of
Jigsaw traps that share

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.640
the same concept of drowning
or smothering the victim

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.320
if they can't complete their task on time,

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.130
from the pig vat in Saw 3,
to the water cube in Saw 5,

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:25.500
to the silo in Jigsaw.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.090
With those that he has deemed
to be bad cops out of the way,

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:30.900
Schenk is ready for the finale
of his plan and kidnaps Zeke,

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:32.490
who awakens chained to a pipe.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.080
Obviously this is a
callback to the beginning

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.850
of the first movie, so I'm
not even going to count that

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.450
on the Things You Missed,
but after escaping,

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:38.660
he discovers Pete, who is caught

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:41.180
in the last Jigsaw-inspired
trap, the glass grinder.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.590
This one is just like the
freezer room from Saw 3.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.330
The only difference is
that instead of the victim

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.700
being sprayed with freezing water

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.490
while someone tries to find
the key to set them free,

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:50.910
Pete is getting sliced
apart by shards of glass.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:53.210
Schenk's hideout is in an
abandoned soap factory.

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.240
This is another common
trait shared with Jigsaw,

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.510
who always used abandoned
factories as his hideouts

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.270
and locations for his games.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.140
It even has the obligatory sliding door,

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.710
although nobody says the
iconic line, "Game over"

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:04.543
in this one.

00:13:04.543 --> 00:13:06.600
The final trap is the puppet
trap, where Chief Banks

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.510
is hung up on strings,
and Zeke must decide

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.510
between saving him or killing Schenk.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.850
This one isn't really
inspired by a Jigsaw trap,

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:13.683
from what I can tell,

00:13:13.683 --> 00:13:16.310
but the explanation of the
trap is definitely familiar.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:17.630
Roll it.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.040
- You know there's 1.5 gallons of blood

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.510
in the human body, Zeke?

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.068
At the rate your old man's bleeding out,

00:13:22.068 --> 00:13:25.469
in just three minutes,
those jars will be full,

00:13:25.469 --> 00:13:28.110
and your dad will be dead.

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:28.943
- The human body, for instance,

00:13:28.943 --> 00:13:32.000
is an astoundingly durable creation.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.950
It contains approximately
10 pints of blood,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.970
yet it is still able to
operate with just half of that.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:42.350
- 10 pints is only equal to 1.25 gallons.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.240
I looked it up, and I
guess the average body has

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.800
1.2 to 1.5 gallons of blood,
so they're kind of both right,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:48.633
in a way.

00:13:48.633 --> 00:13:50.340
This trap not only takes
place behind this wall

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.000
graffitied with Jigsaw's spiral symbol,

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.120
but it's also located at the
top of a spiral staircase.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:56.210
Well, kind of.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:59.160
After a classic Saw-style
montage, Detective Schenk,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.500
or if we're calling him by his
real name, William Emerson,

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:04.166
slips away in an elevator,
just as Jigsaw did way back

00:14:04.166 --> 00:14:07.330
in Darren Lynn Bousman's
first Saw movie, Saw 2.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:08.980
I want to give a big thanks to Lionsgate

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.290
for sponsoring this video.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:11.460
Check out the link in the description

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.087
to purchase your own copy of Spiral.

00:14:13.087 --> 00:14:15.250
But to fully understand the world of Saw,

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.990
you'll need to click
that playlist on the left

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.460
for Thing You Missed episodes
on every single movie.

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:22.530
Remember, subscribe to CZsWorld
for new horrors every week,

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.680
and ring that death bell
for all notifications,

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:29.255
and I'll see you in the next
one, assuming we both survive.

